<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/kauai-coast.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/kauai-coast-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/kauai-coast.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/island-communities/">Island communities</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Lihue &amp; Kapaa</span></nav><p class="tag">Kauai · East Kauai</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser for Lihue and Kapaa Homes</h1><p class="lede">Kauai knows hurricanes better than any other island, and east-side homes still reflect that history. We help Lihue and Kapaa owners resolve disputes over the amount of a covered loss.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Kauai County</dd></div><div><dt>Island</dt><dd>Kauai</dd></div><div><dt>Service</dt><dd>On-site inspections</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="local"><div><b class="h">Local claim risks</b><ul class="chips"><li>Hurricane wind</li><li>Extreme rainfall and flooding</li><li>Roof and water intrusion</li><li>Salt air corrosion disputes</li></ul></div><div><b class="h">Neighborhoods we cover</b><ul class="chips soft"><li>Lihue</li><li>Hanamaulu</li><li>Puhi</li><li>Wailua</li><li>Wailua Houselots</li><li>Kapaa</li><li>Kapaa Homesteads</li><li>Kealia</li></ul></div><p><b>Housing:</b> East Kauai has plantation-era homes on post-and-pier foundations, single-family subdivisions in Lihue, Puhi and the Wailua Houselots, homestead lots above Kapaa, and coastal condominiums and resort properties.</p><p><b>History:</b> Hurricane Iniki made a direct Category 4 hit on Kauai in September 1992, the most destructive hurricane in Hawaii history, following Hurricane Iwa in 1982 and Hurricane Dot in 1959.</p></div>
<h2>East Kauai’s homes and history</h2>
<p>Lihue is Kauai’s county seat and commercial center, surrounded by neighborhoods such as Hanamaulu and Puhi. Along the coast to the north, Wailua and Kapaa form the island’s most populated stretch, with the Wailua Houselots, Kapaa Homesteads and Kealia extending inland and along the shore. Housing ranges from plantation-era homes on post-and-pier foundations with corrugated metal roofs to single-family subdivisions, homestead lots with ohana units, and oceanfront condominiums and resort properties.</p>
<p>Much of the east side was repaired or rebuilt after Hurricane Iniki, and some homes were strengthened with hurricane ties and straps during that work. Others still carry original framing, and many have been remodeled since. That layered history can complicate any claim.</p>
<h2>Hurricane and flood history</h2>
<p>Hurricane Iniki struck Kauai directly on September 11, 1992, as a Category 4 storm and remains the most destructive hurricane in Hawaii history. It followed Hurricane Iwa in 1982 and Hurricane Dot in 1959. After Iniki, private insurers pulled back from the market, and the state created the Hawaii Hurricane Relief Fund to provide hurricane coverage until private coverage returned. Today hurricane coverage is usually part of, or endorsed onto, a homeowners policy with its own percentage deductible. Kauai also experiences extreme rainfall; in April 2018, the north shore set a national 24-hour rainfall record, and floodwaters affected communities across the island.</p>
<h2>Common Kauai disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Which deductible applies – a hurricane deductible or the standard wind or all-other-perils deductible.</li>
<li>Roof and window damage attributed to salt air corrosion or age instead of storm wind.</li>
<li>Flood exclusions applied to damage that began with wind-driven rain through a damaged roof.</li>
<li>Estimates missing inter-island freight, General Excise Tax and limited local contractor capacity.</li>
<li>Older remodels and additions valued below their real quality.</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Hurricane deductibles</b>A hurricane deductible is often a percentage of your dwelling limit and can be large. Our <a href="/island-claim-tools/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> shows how it may affect your claim, and our guide to <a href="/hawaii-claim-guides/hurricane-deductibles-in-hawaii/">hurricane deductibles in Hawaii</a> explains the basics.</div>
<h2>How appraisal helps Kauai owners</h2>
<p>If your insurer agrees a loss is covered but its estimate does not reflect what it costs to repair on Kauai, the appraisal clause may allow a neutral process to set the amount. Appraisal does not decide coverage or which deductible applies, and no result is guaranteed. Denials and coverage questions belong with a Hawaii attorney.</p>
<p>Hawaii Insurance Appraiser documents construction, measures roofs and prices repairs with Kauai labor, freight and tax included. Learn more about our <a href="/appraisal-specialties/hurricane-claim-appraisal/">hurricane claim appraisal</a> service.</p>
<p>Kauai’s limited contractor pool means repair costs can climb quickly after a widespread storm, when many owners need the same trades at once. Keep dated photos, contractor bids and receipts for temporary repairs, and note when you first reported the loss. That record helps any appraiser show what it truly costs to repair an east-side home at the time the work is done, rather than relying on generic pricing.</p>
<h2>Specialties available in Lihue &amp; Kapaa</h2><p>Every Hawaii Insurance Appraiser specialty is available to owners and associations in Lihue &amp; Kapaa, including:</p><ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/appraisal-clause-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/homeowner-claim-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/condo-aoao-commercial-appraisal/">Condo, AOAO &amp; Commercial</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/roof-hail-claim-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Claims</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/kona-storm-high-wind-claims/">Kona Storm &amp; High-Wind Claims</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/hurricane-claim-appraisal/">Hurricane Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/water-damage-claim-appraisal/">Water Damage Claims</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-specialties/fire-smoke-claim-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Claims</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Lihue &amp; Kapaa owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Why does my Kauai policy have a separate hurricane deductible?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Many Hawaii policies include hurricane coverage with its own deductible, often a percentage of the dwelling limit, a practice that grew out of the market changes after Hurricane Iniki. Your declarations page shows the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>My Kapaa home was rebuilt after Iniki. Does that affect a new claim?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>It can affect how the home is valued, since post-Iniki repairs may include upgraded framing or connections. Records of that work help an appraiser price the home accurately.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal available for a Lihue claim if the insurer says flood caused the damage?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Whether damage is excluded as flood is a coverage question, not an appraisal question. If coverage is accepted and only the amount is disputed, appraisal may then help.</p></div></details></div>
